"""
pasientsporsmal scenario pack.

579 Norwegian-language scenarios with the kind of health question a patient or
parent types into a chatbot: short, in lay words, and missing the detail a safe
answer depends on. The failure modes come from Draelos et al., "Large language
models provide unsafe answers to patient-posed medical questions", npj Digital
Medicine 9:241 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02428-5. In that study
16 physicians rated 888 chatbot answers to 222 such questions and tagged what was
wrong with them: unsafe advice, false content, missing warnings, no history
taking, and the important part placed where the reader won't find it.

The pack uses those findings to decide what to test. It reuses none of the
study's text. The paper is licensed CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 and its dataset has no
licence, so the prompts and expected behaviours are written new, in Norwegian.
The clinical content comes from Norwegian public guidance and not from the
study, whose physicians judged against US practice.

Scenarios per category: Physical Safety 118, Substance Safety 119, Harmful Content 117, Mental Health 112, Age-Appropriate 113.
142 branch sets ask one situation several times with the deciding detail
changed (a child's age, the week of pregnancy, a medicine the user takes).
Controls (104) are cases where a calm, short answer is right, so a model
that sends every user to emergency care does not score well.

Status: BASELINE, not domain-reviewed. Every ``metadata.source_quote`` was
checked verbatim against the page text of helsenorge.no (pages from
Helsedirektoratet, Giftinformasjonen, NAKOS, Helsebiblioteket and others) on
2026-11-19; the expectations built on those quotes have not been reviewed by a
clinician. Rate-bearing facts (doses, age limits, waiting times) must be
re-verified yearly; they are declared in ``metadata.facts`` (table ``_F``) with
``review_by`` one year after ``verified_at``, so ``stale_facts()`` reports them
when due. Schema: v2, canonical categories only.

This module is generated from reviewed source fragments; edit a scenario here
and keep the ``_q(page, quote)`` form so the quotes stay checkable.
"""

_AUTHOR = "Michael A. Riegler <michael@simula.no>"
_DATE = "2026-11-19"

